                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} add : \mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi add
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} add : \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
          %p
            and then when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi add
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 3
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} add(3,2)
            is invoked, the compiler knows to call the first function since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi add
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 4.2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} add(4.2,\pi)
            is invoked it calls the second function since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 4.2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4.2
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
          %p
            Note that there is nothing which stipulates that the
            %em behavior
            of a class of ad-hocly polymorphic functions with the same name should be at all similar. Nothing prevents us from defining
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi add
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} add : \mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
            to add its arguments but
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi add
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} add : \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
            to subtract its arguments. Of course, it is good programming practice to make overloaded functions similar in their behavior.
          %p
            In the example above, there might even be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coercion"} coercion
            function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+diagram"} commutative diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℕ
                          %mo ×
                          %mi ℕ
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi add
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℕ
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %mi c
                              %mo ×
                              %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mi c
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mo ×
                          %mi ℝ
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mi add
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array {
                  \\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N}     &amp; \overset{add}\to  &amp; \mathbb{N} \\
                  \\mathllap{c \times c} \downarrow &amp;                   &amp; \downarrow \mathrlap{c} \\
                  \\mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}     &amp; \underset{add}\to &amp; \mathbb{R}
                  }
          %p But things don't always work out this way.
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typecase"} typecase
          %h2#parametric_polymorphism Parametric polymorphism
          %p
            In
            %strong parametric polymorphism
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %strong parametricity
            = succeed "," do
              %em once
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+variable"} type variable
            that can be instantiated at many different types to produce different functions. For instance, we can define a function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi first
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} first : A\times A \to A
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a type variable (or
            = succeed ")," do
              %em parameter
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi first
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} first(x,y) \coloneqq x.
          %p Now the compiler automatically instantiates a copy of this function, with identical code, for any type at which it is called. Thus we can behave as if we had functions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi first
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} first : \mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi first
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} first : \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
          %p
            and so on, for any types we wish. In contrast to
            %em ad hoc
            polymorphism, in this case we do have a guarantee that all these same-named functions are doing “the same thing”, because they are all instantiated by the same original polymorphic code.
          %p
            Parametric polymorphism makes sense and is very useful in languages with non-dependent type systems, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haskell"} Haskell
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Standard ML
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Standard+ML"} ?
          %h3#theorems_for_free Theorems for Free
          %p
            In type systems with parametric polymorphism, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/System+F"} System F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times B \to A
            has the
            %span.newWikiWord
              free theorem
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/free+theorem"} ?
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi α
                  %mo ×
                  %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \circ f = f \circ (\alpha \times \beta)
          %p
            for all functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            with that signature (
            %a{:href => "#Wadler89"}> Wadler 89
            , figure 1).
          %h3#in_dependent_type_theory In dependent type theory
          %p
            In a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} type universe
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+variables"} type variables
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi Type
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} first
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} or
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} first
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma, A:Type \vdash \mathrm{first}(A):A\times A \to A \qquad \mathrm{or} \qquad \Gamma, A \; \mathrm{type} \vdash \mathrm{first}(A): A \times A \to A
          %p
            If the dependent type theory additionally has universe-indexed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+types"} dependent product types
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/impredicative+polymorphism"} impredicative polymorphism
            respectively, a parametrically polymorphic family of functions can simply be considered to be a single dependently typed function whose first argument is a type. Thus our function above would be typed as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} first
                  %mo :
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} or
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} first
                  %mo :
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{first} : \prod_{A:Type} A\times A \to A \qquad \mathrm{or} \qquad \mathrm{first} : \prod_{A \; \mathrm{type}} A\times A \to A
          %p
            Examples of parametric polymorphism in dependent type theory include
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+schemata"} axiom schemata
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UIP"} UIP
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+K"} axiom K
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theoretic+axiom+of+replacement"} axiom of replacement
            for a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tarski+universe"} Tarski universe
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typal+equality"} typal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursion"} recursion principle
            of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"}> higher
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+types"} inductive types
            that shows it to be a homotopy-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typal+equality"} typal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/corecursion"} corecursion principle
            of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+coinductive+type"}> higher
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coinductive+types"} coinductive types
            that shows it to be a homotopy-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"}> terminal object
            \.
          %p
            Many
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theories"} dependent type theories
            satisfy a meta-theoretic version of parametric polymorphism. However, there are some dependent type theories which do not have parametric polymorphism in the meta-theoretic sense, such as dependent type theories with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typecase"} typecase
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BPT17"} Boulier, Pédrot &amp; Tabareau 2017
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parametric+dependent+type+theories"} parametric dependent type theories
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+type+theory"} simple type theory
                %li
                  %p
                    %strong polymorphic type theory
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polymorphic+dependent+type+theory"} polymorphic dependent type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+relations"} logical relations
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/impredicative+polymorphism"} impredicative polymorphism
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parametric+dependent+type+theory"} parametric dependent type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typecase"} typecase
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The distinction between ad hoc and parametric polymorphism is originally due to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christopher+Strachey"} Christopher Strachey
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christopher+Strachey"}> Christopher Strachey
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fundamental concepts in programming languages
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.itu.dk/courses/BPRD/E2009/fundamental-1967.pdf"} pdf
          %p Other classic papers include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+C.+Reynolds"}> John C. Reynolds
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Towards a Theory of Type Structure
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://repository.cmu.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=2289&context=compsci"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robin+Milner"}> Robin Milner
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Theory of Type Polymorphism in Programming
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://courses.engr.illinois.edu/cs421/sp2013/project/milner-polymorphism.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+C.+Reynolds"}> John C. Reynolds
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Types, Abstraction, and Parametric Polymorphism
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.cse.chalmers.se/edu/year/2010/course/DAT140_Types/Reynolds_typesabpara.pdf"} pdf
          %p Theorems for free were explained in:
          %ul
            %li#Wadler89
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Wadler"}> Philip Wadler
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Theorems for free!
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://www2.cs.sfu.ca/CourseCentral/831/burton/Notes/July14/free.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1145/99370.99404"} doi:10.1145/99370.99404
          %p Some discussion on parametricity and polymorphism appears in:
          %ul
            %li#CTZulip
              %p
                %em> Continuations, parametricity, and polymorphism
                , Category Theory Zulip (
                %a{:href => "https://categorytheory.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/229199-learning.3A-questions/topic/Continuations.2C.20parametricity.2C.20and.20polymorphism/with/527734153"}> web
                )
            %li#BPT17
              %p
                Simon Boulier, Pierre-Marie Pédrot, Nicolas Tabareau,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The next 700 syntactical models of type theory
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1145/3018610.3018620"} doi:10.1145/3018610.3018620
          %p
            See also the references at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parametric+dependent+type+theory"} parametric dependent type theory
            for the various notions of parametricity in various kinds of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theories"} dependent type theories
        .revisedby
